#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/93c4cdd7a16be0f08bd161b97dde5002a3f12dee2febd3857fe9da289f9a9c4a.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/7102b8bb9499f228ac58b16b7fd977fbc2d95e1ef87c21742f14255d4cdbc992.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124279 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069543/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68fb280e-41bd-58de-b8fb-7a94029b73b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361650} {:answer/id #uuid "8746d15a-9c6d-561f-a33b-c1fd942129a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361651} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfff2b4-7787-59d5-96da-1b6fddf6b7d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361652} {:answer/id #uuid "07eb84d4-f94a-5668-992e-4902a708b4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361653} {:answer/id #uuid "f187e9c7-f3d2-59a8-a817-fa5e098e6b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f187e9c7-f3d2-59a8-a817-fa5e098e6b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361654} :answer-field/id #uuid "813f3453-52e4-5a3f-9bdd-5f75f0a28817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9130178-79b6-5431-9c03-0a59d7f3d7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140519" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=4+1=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7b08d2-6aa3-571d-bce0-f9f5b6834e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361657} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e22241-9ebb-5abe-926d-904c3d94a437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I-\\frac{1}{6}A+\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361658} {:answer/id #uuid "16023665-e33b-5d9a-afa5-f419d0d7b5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{6}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361659} {:answer/id #uuid "8b85c38f-37a1-53a0-b59f-11216cdc771e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{12}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361660} {:answer/id #uuid "64c0d39d-9c35-5ced-96c0-3594d2ccc28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}I+\\frac{1}{3}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c7b08d2-6aa3-571d-bce0-f9f5b6834e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361657} :answer-field/id #uuid "846946f0-267b-5b49-9625-b464e537521c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7a4964c-4a39-54df-8b48-fb778fd1712f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140531" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-2A-11I+24{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 24{A}^{-1} & =11I+2A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17b47d-b289-5872-b063-cf53aa0ef264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361664} {:answer/id #uuid "6427f3a0-f9fa-5d04-b3ac-d52371b10fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30A+15I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361665} {:answer/id #uuid "64da6072-9f34-5c90-9583-bf4f2a0a2002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-39A+22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361666} {:answer/id #uuid "a9025c5f-3c9e-5657-a5ec-fe6c146a1c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12A+53I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361667} {:answer/id #uuid "08009902-dd94-5db6-8af9-7bb44310d206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21A+2I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64da6072-9f34-5c90-9583-bf4f2a0a2002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-39A+22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361666} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae99cdb-60a7-53e7-b946-5dba77a360d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed875ee1-48ba-51f2-ad97-578fc7e1d457" :question/math-academy-id "q-140606" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $3x$ | $+$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+3x-2\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $9{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $33x$ | $-$ | $22$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $39x$ | $+$ | $22$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-39x+22),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-3x+11.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =-39A+22I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723b0dd9-698e-58be-86a4-da19d64d080d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A-16I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361671} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5db555-5134-5f04-91bc-70ec8767e8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361672} {:answer/id #uuid "38160ace-c173-5622-8bf6-d410bbcfc6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6A-7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361673} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbdf807-9ff9-5011-84de-ab72dfb1d527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7A+6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361674} {:answer/id #uuid "6d49c8f9-4d86-5ee7-9c3d-1332e50fe107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7A-6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d49c8f9-4d86-5ee7-9c3d-1332e50fe107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7A-6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361675} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc446cea-dcb4-584a-bf51-93ea58549a14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d68e0e9a-eb2c-51e0-8ff2-f6c527adfad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37773" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $-$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+x-6\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $+$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $+$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x-1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =7A-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9240c62-1467-5833-b3d1-0d888c003822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{4}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3d890a-ac9a-51e8-b2cf-20e316b06fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} {:answer/id #uuid "8aeac5df-3477-5833-bd74-77b4b093bac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{1}{5}A+\\frac{2}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335729} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3bf075-052d-564f-a254-b01064ab26c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5I-4A-5{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335730} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb5465e-a77c-5890-93ee-60d0a6082d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2I+4A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d3d890a-ac9a-51e8-b2cf-20e316b06fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b2e733-2ac0-5ba5-9637-8154cf7d6a7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544f8b26-26dd-59f6-ae3d-e40288df33a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37831" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89783926-4cfe-56f9-81e9-4436b84ed5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123155} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba69863-39c5-5a32-9dc4-623af9bea747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123156} {:answer/id #uuid "e6da4d25-bbba-59e6-ad91-4857751e5dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123157} {:answer/id #uuid "042ad365-cce6-50fa-be38-2c6b05793a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123158} {:answer/id #uuid "77c3f60f-212c-502f-bf4f-155190c198cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6da4d25-bbba-59e6-ad91-4857751e5dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123157} :answer-field/id #uuid "a34e849a-2e62-5b3c-a693-e5319def7679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9892d6a4-00e5-53a4-a283-14b8510646e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37835" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-1\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{22}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{20}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $3x$ | $-$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7be6c6-77ce-5830-a27b-e4e87375e74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0d3e9b-c681-55da-8838-335af666adc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735} {:answer/id #uuid "a36b5ef8-f1ee-5a49-9b22-a8fd509dbce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335736} {:answer/id #uuid "47f091bc-9f0a-5a78-81b9-2cbcfeaf6b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335737} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e839bf-4ae5-50b5-bdc4-f1d49eb6db2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa0d3e9b-c681-55da-8838-335af666adc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffdc1c38-f4aa-53b1-baca-a1de7440c70b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f265b9ef-7603-5f92-bdb0-6bd466019f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-38703" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69e716c-5efe-50f3-8ce6-8d29dedfb6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123353} {:answer/id #uuid "bb300bb4-db72-562f-a51e-14d9205d0480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123354} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d24d29-2df6-518a-ac2f-5863f4812ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123355} {:answer/id #uuid "5822a9f6-39af-5eb1-ada8-369b1c2d3cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123356} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd6099b-3374-5305-9570-e34559846da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb300bb4-db72-562f-a51e-14d9205d0480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123354} :answer-field/id #uuid "99ecbdb3-da6f-5be5-b816-69e54b42c015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f0cac47-d84c-5a80-b1f4-a28ee45248f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-38720" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=-12+3=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db10fe87-4080-51de-81c0-15a642191abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361678} {:answer/id #uuid "222aa5b5-eb2f-5362-bffe-6ddf95ce05f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361679} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd1e470-e2e8-51f8-bac3-5bae9ed53e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2I-A-2{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361680} {:answer/id #uuid "27704e3e-cb96-5838-9059-1fd29d8363bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{7}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361681} {:answer/id #uuid "241ae2fa-cdf3-5aad-81c7-7048c5a0658a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222aa5b5-eb2f-5362-bffe-6ddf95ce05f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361679} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ff4040-4ecd-5896-9de2-3cf9ce932e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ef480b2-61d6-5846-831c-c9cbf5e226fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40168" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74263b8a-e843-5b1e-9f7d-ba1add9ae7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335776} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ab2057-27d7-556d-95c1-15c27ec91175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335777} {:answer/id #uuid "7b32cda2-edd7-5d90-bae0-f8834606abd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335778} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0f81f4-d420-5154-9b57-a836739903f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335779} {:answer/id #uuid "28e9b74f-4942-588d-8846-8ec8f80d2247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ab2057-27d7-556d-95c1-15c27ec91175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335777} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5f2fe2-d4c8-5427-9ee6-71a6b2494263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cebfd11-81a6-5d58-a074-6a39a1866435" :question/math-academy-id "q-83393" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =10-24 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c327c4a-e6c3-5406-87de-ac028fbf7d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7A-17I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a7b3bc-90a7-5a0a-a700-0da7c401873f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17A-7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335784} {:answer/id #uuid "610b94ee-1e37-59f1-8b69-caa28545842a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335785} {:answer/id #uuid "314dbd11-698b-59fc-a877-3b8b6cc12187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7A-17I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8106da-ae0e-5950-bf39-b84557225cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7A+17I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c327c4a-e6c3-5406-87de-ac028fbf7d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7A-17I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c979d9b-fd19-50ea-a891-8dd2764138af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bcdb60e-6a7a-590a-bbcb-250cd0fb068c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83396" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=-7$ and $\\text{det}(A)=17.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-7A-17I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71321766-3826-5546-833e-67129eb1ec2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{3}-10{A}^{2}+12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361685} {:answer/id #uuid "76afaa67-6f51-54da-8606-09c192f75504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361686} {:answer/id #uuid "24567a86-5e3d-568b-aef1-5270225fc6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-2I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361687} {:answer/id #uuid "11337e49-e829-51f3-bd54-59e3dd281638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-10I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361688} {:answer/id #uuid "125eb794-22ad-5c6d-8f24-077d5fb930dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{3}-{A}^{2}-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76afaa67-6f51-54da-8606-09c192f75504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361686} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9556e8-ca13-50b6-b8a0-1f48264210af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "901a475b-0f26-5be8-adcb-f752bbed2b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-83397" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 4$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}+2{\\lambda }^{3}-10{\\lambda }^{2}+12.$ Then, ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{4}+2{A}^{3}-10{A}^{2}+12I & =O \\\\ {A}^{4} & =-2{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-12I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fce000-0a06-56c6-b926-11b3d93b2f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36A-14I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335790} {:answer/id #uuid "4e530648-6e48-5088-b0df-62ff267bb131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14A-36I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335791} {:answer/id #uuid "99e56666-aa8a-5ed4-a1d5-a5abbb0dee05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14A+36I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335792} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc97aa6-3bdc-53dc-8aef-ee1a9118426b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36A+14I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335793} {:answer/id #uuid "a086fcce-d41c-55ba-86fa-47a07c488c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14A-36I" 